<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Vietnam</h1><p class="st-meta">1955 CE – 1975 CE · capital Saigon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">South Vietnam, the US-backed anti-communist state of 1955 to 1975, whose fall to North Vietnamese forces ended the Vietnam War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In October 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Ngo Dinh Diem</a>, prime minister of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>, deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai</a> through a referendum and proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a> with himself as president. It controlled the territory south of the 17th parallel, with nearly a million northern refugees, many Catholic, among its people.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Diem</a>'s government, dominated by his family, was authoritarian and repressed communists, sects and, in 1963, Buddhist protesters. It fought a growing insurgency led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">National Liberation Front</a>. After Diem's overthrow and murder in November 1963, a series of generals ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-van-thieu">Nguyen Van Thieu</a> became president in 1967. More than half a million US troops fought in the country by 1968.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a> of 1973 US forces withdrew, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">North Vietnam</a>'s spring offensive of 1975 overran the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> fell on 30 April 1975. Authority passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-south-vietnam">Republic of South Vietnam</a>, and many South Vietnamese fled abroad as boat people.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-vietnam.jpg" alt="Republic of Vietnam" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: (many, see history) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Vietnam.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1955 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saigon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ngo Dinh Diem, Nguyen Van Thieu, Duong Van Minh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>South Vietnam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1965&amp;focus=republic-of-vietnam" aria-label="Republic of Vietnam on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7619 1775 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Vietnam lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7939 2031L7939 2031M7939 2031L7846 1656M7939 2031L7938 2033"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7939 2031h0M7846 1656h0M7938 2033h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7939 2031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7939 2031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saigon. <a href="/atlas?year=1965&amp;focus=republic-of-vietnam">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
